          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "# multiply(*) will happen first as it has higher precedence and + has lower precedence\n",
        "expr = 10 + 20 * 30\n",
        "print(expr)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "GTG6KOkdgJE9"
      },
      "source": [
        "Logical operators (and, or, not) also follow precedence.\n",
        "* precedence order:  NOT -> AND -> OR\n",
        "* e.g., I will go if it doesn't(NOT) rain AND I finish work OR have a car. (Follows natural language logic)\n",
        "([click for revision](https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/python/precedence-and-associativity-of-operators-in-python/))"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"
        },
        "id": "H7NLPO5JdIqU",
        "outputId": "b85a047f-4ffc-48ba-8225-bdeaacc3a2a0"
      },
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "Hello! Welcome.\n"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "# Precedence of 'or' & 'and'\n",
        "name = \"Alex\"\n",
        "age = 0\n",
        "if name == \"Alex\" or (name == \"John\" and age >= 2):\n",
        "    print(\"Hello! Welcome.\")\n",
        "else:\n",
        "    print(\"Good Bye!!\")\n",
        "\n",
        "# name == \"John\" and age >= 2 is checked first, evaluates to False\n",
        "# since name == \"Alex\" is True, the condition passes\n",
        "\n",
        "# This will evaluate same even if () is removed bcoz it follows the logical operator precedence, i.e AND first, OR after.\n",
        "# Good practice to write () for better undersanding and readibility of code."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "H8Y25NY8kGbv"
      },
      "source": [
        "***Operators Associativity:***\n",
        "* It defines the evaluation direction for operators of equal precedence in an expression.\n",
        "* Most operators evaluate left to right (+, -, *, /, %, AND, OR)\n",
        "* NOT is excluded as it is a unary operator acting on a single operand, not connecting two operands like other operators. NOT simply reverses the truth value of a single condition. Therefore it doesn't have a direction of associativity.\n",
        "* Exponentiation and assignment group evaluates right to left (**, =, +=, -=, *=, and /=)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"
        },
        "id": "HdZ7MGUWheNF",
        "outputId": "d2fe64ec-251e-4ffa-883d-f45f3b1386c8"
      },
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "250.0\n"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "exp = 250 / 10 * 10\n",
        "print(exp)\n",
        "\n",
        "# '/' is evaluated first as it is the leftmost\n",
        "# '*' is evaluated after '/'\n",
        "# / & * both have same precedence but Left to Right (LTR) associativity"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "hSCU_dslhn49"
      },
      "source": [
        "***Examples of LTR associativity for Multiplication, Division, Addition and Substraction***"
